Hello! It's been a bit since I last reflected in this journal. Today, I read Chapter 2 if Don Norman's The Design of Everyday Things. This chapter explored more of the human mind in relation to how things are done, how it makes them feel, and the methodology of their actions. It also talks about people being storytellers, which is cool.
I found the short exerpt at the beginning of the chapter to be a cute exchange. Initially, I thought the old mechanical draw was just rusty and needed to be replaced or something. I appreciated that Norman broke it down further later.
As people, we blame ourselves for fear of lack of understanding and seeming foolish, like the landlady did with the non-traditional drawer. I'd assume if it's mechanical, that it's not wooden, it's something new and foreign to an older person. 

Having dealt with helping my parents (almost 60-70 years old), and their lack of confidence with technology and machines can be both endearing and infuriating. (like using the folder function on a desktop or seatching google on an Ipad, and seeing how they refuse to swap the paperwork in their car business to online documentation because it's too new and could get cyberhacked...)

A few new terms or phrases is The Gulf of Execution and The Gulf of Evaluation. From what I can understand, it's like a series of feedback loops, where we have to be mindful of whether or not something is working the way it should or how we'd expect, whether a proper interaction worked the way we'd want or expect, and if not then how we could fix it. 
So yeah, evaluating, assessing, problem solving. Norman feels many people don't conduct the problem solving or fail to understand how to solve their issues, then blame themselves for believed ignorance.

With proper signifiers, why is it still so common for people to just give up? Maybe laziness haha or not enough reward for wokring to complete the objective, like a videogame. 

Norman explores The Seven Stages of Action
Oh cool, he mentioned how the two parts of an action are executing and evaluating. They're both influenced by and can influence our emotions. 
The seven stage action cycle is: goal, plan, specify, perform, percieve, interpret, and compare. 
More affirming for my previous thoughts- Norman calls this a feedback loop ૮ ˶ᵔ ᵕ ᵔ˶ ა

I liked how on page 43, Norman mentions that "the seven stages provide a guidline for developing new products or services" and the gulfs provide places to start. It calls to mind the importance of conducting user research, surveys, interviews, etc to create and improve products and designs.

Another interesting part was how Norman explained the processing behind the Subconcious with the alternating finger move example. The subconcious follows patterns or trends instictually, while the concious takes some time to logically process it, then execute a varied response. Then our emotions make things a little sillier.

Some other ideas mentioned were the 3 levels of processing: visceral(precursor to emotion, fast), behavioral(learned skills), and reflective (conscious cognition) levels of processing. Pretty straight forward. I think that further reinforces the idea I had earlier that people may not put in effort to problem solve because, they don't see value in doing it, or not a good enough reward for the effort. They have too many negative conscious and subconcious biases toward that thing, which Norman says having a positive attitude is vital for creativity. And creativity is crucial to problem solving. 
Oh my gosh, I'm realizing that some of this has applied to me and my approach to coding, specifically with my last project and one I had over the summer. I can work through it, the imposter syndrom I guess, and have many times, but lately I guess the creativity and drive to problem solve with coding in P5.js and Visual Studio, and even with just drawing has been almost nonexistent for me. I haven't been motivated to sit for a couple hours to learn and even relearn some key parts, or figure out how to make something look or work better. Rambly journal talk...
Essentially, in terms of creativity and problem solving with art and coding, my viseral reaction has been "oh no, that takes so much time. I don't feel productive when I do this. I don't know what I'm doing". Then I productively procrastinate on other things, usually helping, caring for people or something I can actually control or zoning out with a game... Then reflective, I'll end up feeling bad and anxious. 

Reading over the rest of the chapter provided some more insight into why we go through these patterns
